\begin{abstract}
We present a real-time astrophysical refraction engine capable of simulating 
multi-index refractive fields under extreme computational loads while maintaining 
deterministic correctness. The system integrates GPU-accelerated eikonal solvers, 
sparse voxel octrees, multi-wavelength dispersion sampling, and a Closed-Loop 
Deterministic Invariant Verifier (CL-DIV). CL-DIV enforces bitwise determinism, 
numerical stability, and cryptographically auditable correctness across all hardware 
layers. This work introduces a new class of scientific instrument for real-time 
astrophysical lensing analysis and anomaly detection.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Astrophysical refraction phenomena typically require offline computation due to the 
complexity of multi-index propagation, gravitational lensing fields, and plasma 
dispersion. This paper introduces a real-time architecture capable of resolving 
chromatic anomalies across $280$--$1900$ nm while maintaining deterministic correctness 
through CL-DIV. The engine is designed to operate under extreme stress conditions, 
including volumetric density spikes, ray saturation, bandwidth flooding, and 
near-singular gradient injection.

\section{Mathematical Framework}

The engine solves the scalar eikonal equation:
\[
|\nabla \Phi| = n(\mathbf{x}),
\]
where $\Phi$ is the optical path function and $n(\mathbf{x})$ is the effective refractive index:
\[
n_{\text{eff}}(r,\lambda) = n_g(r) + n_p(\lambda) + n_{\text{exo}}(r,\lambda).
\]

Ray propagation is computed via RK4 integration:
\[
\mathbf{x}'(s) = \frac{1}{n_{\text{eff}}} \nabla \Phi.
\]

